---
id: org.dxos.spec.plugin
name: DSL.Plugin
version: 1.1
extends:
  - org.dxos.spec.core
---

The plugin dialect: one `plugin` block that names a Composer plugin, says what it is for, and lists
the modules it contributes. Before this dialect a plugin was implied by its document (`PLUGIN.mdl`)
and its frontmatter `id`; the block makes the plugin an item the spec can describe, review and link
to like any other.

It is deliberately small. A module is listed by name with one line of prose; the capabilities it
provides, when it activates and what it requires are left to the code and to later versions of the
dialect. Use the app dialect (`app.mdl`) for the plugin's UI: graph nodes, surfaces and menus.

## Extensions

| Term     | URI                        |
| -------- | -------------------------- |
| `plugin` | `org.dxos.mdl.plugin@1.1` |

## Model

- A plugin spec opens with its `plugin` block, directly after the frontmatter and before any
  prose, so the first thing a reader sees is what the plugin is and what it adds. A document
  declares at most one, and its identifier is the plugin's class name (`CodePlugin`). `id:`
  repeats the frontmatter `id`, so the block can be quoted on its own.
- `dependsOn:` is optional and lists the plugin ids this plugin needs at runtime, exactly as
  `dx.config.ts` declares them. Each is also the `id` in that plugin's `PLUGIN.mdl` frontmatter.
  It is written inline (`dependsOn: [a, b]`) or as a block list (`dependsOn:` then indented
  `- id` lines, as `modules:`). Specs it only builds on or refers to go under `uses` instead.
- `modules:` lists every module the plugin adds (`Plugin.addModule` in its `plugin.ts`). Each
  item is the module's export name, a colon, and one line on what it contributes.
- A module named here is a spec item: a review can judge it (`verdict ReactSurface`), and a
  `github:` key may point at it (`…/src/capabilities/index.ts#ReactSurface`).

## Definitions

```mdl
ext plugin
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.plugin@1.1
  desc: |
    A Composer plugin: what it is for and the modules it adds. The identifier is the plugin's
    class name; the inline prose is its purpose in one line.
  fields:
    id: URI                     # the plugin id, as in the document's frontmatter
    desc?: Prose                # what the plugin is for, and what it leaves to other plugins
    dependsOn?: URI[]           # runtime plugin dependencies, as in dx.config.ts
    modules: ProseList          # `- Name: what it contributes`, one per Plugin.addModule
  syntax: |
    plugin CodePlugin: Authors and reviews DEUS specs.
      id: org.dxos.plugin.code
      dependsOn: [org.dxos.plugin.illustrator]
      modules:
        - ReactSurface: the spec article and section surfaces
        - SpecSync: two-way sync of specs with files on disk
```
